@model OptiPowerTools.ScheduledJobsInsights.Cms.ScheduledJobsInsightsPageModel
@* The hosting document, shared by both packages. Each package's Shell.cshtml renders through it and
   supplies only what differs between the CMS 12 and CMS 13 shells: the shell's own resources (the
   ShellResources section) and the navigation it wraps around the page. Everything else - and every
   reason it is the way it is - lives here once. *@
@inject Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.IWebHostEnvironment HostEnvironment
@{
    // The scoped-CSS bundle is named after the entry assembly. ApplicationName usually equals it but
    // is settable independently through WebApplicationOptions, so prefer the assembly name and fall
    // back only when there isn't one.
    var scopedCssName = System.Reflection.Assembly.GetEntryAssembly()?.GetName().Name
        ?? HostEnvironment.ApplicationName;

    // This package's own static web assets are served under _content/{PackageId}, and the CMS 12
    // build ships under a different package id - so the path comes from the build, not a literal.
    var assetBase = OptiPowerTools.ScheduledJobsInsights.Cms.PackageAssets.BasePath;
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>@Model.PageTitle</title>

    @* Required by blazor.server.js: it resolves the /_blazor hub relative to this base. Without it
       the hub is looked up under the current directory — /ScheduledJobsInsightsCms/_blazor — which
       404s, so the circuit never starts. The page then stays as prerendered HTML: the list still
       looks fine, but nothing is interactive and the Virtualize-backed log renders as an empty box.
       Url.Content resolves the app's path base, so this also works under a virtual directory. *@
    <base href="@Url.Content("~/")" />

    @* The shell's own resources, from the package's Shell.cshtml - the one part of the head that
       differs between the CMS 12 and CMS 13 shells. On CMS 12 these are its stylesheets; on CMS 13
       they are the localization data its navigation scripts read (its styling arrives with the
       navigation bundle). Either way the components render inside this document rather than in an
       iframe, so they inherit the shell's styling instead of needing a stand-in stylesheet. *@
    @await RenderSectionAsync("ShellResources", required: true)

    @* Razor scoped CSS (Index.razor.css / Detail.razor.css) is bundled into the *host* application's
       stylesheet, whose name follows the entry assembly - hence resolving it at runtime rather than
       hardcoding a name this package cannot know.

       ApplicationName is not guaranteed to match that bundle: a host can set it explicitly through
       WebApplicationOptions, and the bundle keeps the assembly name. When they differ this link 404s
       and the components render unstyled, so the assembly name is preferred when it is available and
       ApplicationName is only the fallback.

       asp-append-version on both: an upgraded package otherwise serves whatever CSS the browser
       cached from the previous version, which is a support call that looks like a rendering bug. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Url.Content($"~/{scopedCssName}.styles.css")" asp-append-version="true" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Url.Content($"~/{assetBase}/css/scheduled-jobs-insights.css")" asp-append-version="true" />

    @* Records the reader's IANA time zone so the server can render timestamps in it. This runs in the
       document head, before the components below are rendered on any subsequent request, and the
       controller reads it back out of the cookie — which is why it is a cookie rather than JS interop:
       the value has to be available during prerendering, not just once the circuit connects.

       Avoid writing literal HTML tags in these comments: SonarCloud's HTML analyser parses Razor
       comments as markup, so a tag name in here is read as a real element (an extra head element with
       no title, in this case) and reported as a defect in the page.

       Consequence worth knowing: on the very first page view the cookie does not exist yet, so times
       render in UTC (labelled as such, never silently wrong) and every view after that is in the
       reader's own zone. Nothing here reloads the page to avoid that one occurrence. *@
    @* An external file, not inline: an inline script never runs under a script-src policy without
       'unsafe-inline', which is the ordinary case for a CMS back office — and the failure is silent,
       leaving every reader permanently on UTC. asp-append-version for the same cache-busting reason as
       the stylesheets above. *@
    <script src="@Url.Content($"~/{assetBase}/js/timezone.js")" asp-append-version="true" defer></script>
</head>
<body>
    @* platform-navigation advertises an antiforgery header name on this page for the shell's client
       scripts to use, but the token itself has to come from the page. Native CMS pages emit one, so
       this does too — otherwise any shell script that posts from here has no token to send. *@
    @Html.AntiForgeryToken()

    @RenderBody()

    <script src="~/_framework/blazor.server.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
